<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!--
      Ditto is intentionally NOT sandboxed: a clipboard manager that synthesizes
      ⌘V into other apps and registers a global hotkey cannot run under the macOS
      App Sandbox. It ships with the Hardened Runtime (required for notarization);
      Accessibility is granted by the user at runtime and needs no entitlement.
      This file is deliberately minimal — add keys here only if a future feature
      requires them (e.g. com.apple.security.automation.apple-events).
    -->

    <!--
      Data-protection keychain access.

      The legacy file-based keychain guards items with an ACL, and any read by a
      binary not on that ACL needs a confirmation prompt. When no prompt can be
      shown — app relaunched by a script, Mac in dark wake, laptop lid closed —
      the read fails with errSecInDarkWake or errSecInteractionNotAllowed. That is
      not hypothetical: it happened on the developer's own Mac, where the app could
      list all of its keys and read none of them, and older code answered that by
      minting a replacement key over the live one and orphaning the history.

      The data-protection keychain has no ACLs; access is decided by code identity,
      so a correctly-signed app reads its own items with no UI, ever. This
      entitlement is what makes that available.

      IMPORTANT: it only takes effect when the app is signed with a real team
      identity. A self-signed local build has none, so the request fails with
      errSecMissingEntitlement (-34018) and Crypto.storeBlob falls back to the
      legacy keychain — which is why the fallback is load-bearing and not a nicety.
      Release builds signed with a Developer ID get the prompt-free path; developer
      builds keep working exactly as before.
    -->
    <key>keychain-access-groups</key>
    <array>
        <string>$(AppIdentifierPrefix)io.antreas.cliphoard</string>
    </array>
</dict>
</plist>
